</gallery>

== '''Tadozu Kaidō''' ==
{| 
|-
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|319}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Kagawa|number=25}}
|}
A road from {{ill|Tadozu Port|ja|多度津港|12=draft}} to {{ill|Zentsūji City|ja|善通寺市|lt=Zentsūji|12=draft}}, Ikuno, Ōasa, and others leads to Kotohira. Like the Marugame Kaidō, it was frequently used by pilgrims arriving by Konpira ship; however, many of its users came from western Japan and Kyushu. It is said that during the {{ill|Tenpō era|ja|天保|12=draft}}, when the {{ill|Tadozu Domain|ja|多度津藩|12=draft}} undertook a major renovation of Tadozu Port, this route became even busier than the Marugame Kaidō.

The road between Tadozu and Kotohira was constructed as the Shikoku New Road by the politician {{ill|Ōkubo Kennojō|ja|大久保諶之丞|12=draft}} during the Meiji period. The current {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 25|ja|香川県道25号|12=draft}} and {{ill|National Route 319|ja|国道319号|12=draft}} follow its course, running parallel to {{ill|JR Shikoku|ja|四国旅客鉄道|lt=JR Shikoku|12=draft}} and the {{ill|Dosan Line|ja|土讃線|12=draft}}.

== '''Awa Kaidō''' ==
{| 
|-
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|32}}
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|438}}
|}
A road from {{ill|Tokushima Prefecture|ja|徳島県|12=draft}} crossing the {{ill|Sanuki Mountains|ja|讃岐山脈|12=draft}} to Kotohira. The name of Awa-chō within Kotohira Town derives from the large number of visitors from {{ill|Awa Province|ja|阿波国|12=draft}}. Although several highways exist connecting Awa and Sanuki Provinces, the route from {{ill|Miyoshi City|ja|三好市|lt=Awa Ikeda|lt_ja=阿波池田|12=draft}} (displayed as “Awa Ikeda”) via {{ill|Inonohana Pass|ja|猪ノ鼻峠|12=draft}}, Zaitadogawa, and the Mominoki Pass to Kotohira is known as the Awa Betsu Kaidō. This highway was later developed as part of the Shikoku New Road by {{ill|Ōkubo Kennojō|ja|大久保諶之丞|12=draft}} in the Meiji period and subsequently designated as {{ill|National Route 32|ja|国道32号|12=draft}}. Running parallel is the {{ill|JR Shikoku|ja|四国旅客鉄道|12=draft}} Dosan Line.

Pilgrims from Awa, closely associated with Konpira Shrine, often combined their visit with that to {{ill|Hasakura-ji|ja|箸蔵寺|12=draft}} (known as Konpira Oku no-in). The highway connecting Konpira Shrine and Hasakura-ji—not via Inonohana from Zaitada but via Nikenchaya—is known as the Hasakura Kaidō. Although never developed as a vehicular road, the old route has been established as a hiking trail known as {{ill|Shikoku no Michi|ja|四国のみち|12=draft}}.

From {{ill|Tsurugi Town|ja|つるぎ町|lt=Sadamitsu|lt_ja=貞光|12=draft}} (displayed as “Sadamitsu”), across {{ill|Santō Pass|ja|三頭越|lt=Santō Pass|lt_ja=三頭峠|12=draft}}, then through Kubo-ya, Zōda, Shijō, and on to Kotohira, most of the Awa Kaidō was later designated as {{ill|National Route 438|ja|国道438号|12=draft}}. Although the mountain pass section was slow to be modernized for automobiles, in 1997 the {{ill|Santō Tunnel|ja|三頭トンネル|12=draft}} finally opened, easing vehicular traffic. In Mima Town, Mima City, a once-buried torii from the old highway has been revived through local residents’ efforts.<ref>[http://www.topics.or.jp/localNews/news/2011/05/2011_130628816198.html Revival of the Symbolic Torii in Mima Town] (Tokushima Shimbun, May 25, 2011)</ref>

== '''Iyo–Tosa Kaidō''' ==
{| 
|-
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|11}}
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|377}}
|}
A road from {{ill|Ehime Prefecture|ja|愛媛県|12=draft}} along the {{ill|Suihada Coast|ja|燧灘|12=draft}} to Toyohama, then inland via {{ill|Iyo Pass|ja|伊予見峠|12=draft}} to the Ushiyaguchi of Kotohira. In addition to pilgrims from {{ill|Iyo Province|ja|伊予国|lt=Iyo|12=draft}}, many pilgrims from {{ill|Tosa Province|ja|土佐国|lt=Tosa|12=draft}}—arriving via the {{ill|Tosa (North) Kaidō|ja|土佐街道|lt=Tosa (North) Kaidō|12=draft}}—used this route. Many of the lanterns and offerings remaining along the highway were donated by the people of Iyo and Tosa. During the {{ill|Bakumatsu|ja|幕末|12=draft}} period, activists from Tosa (the {{ill|Samurai activists|ja|志士|12=draft}}) also used this road, and a statue of {{ill|Sakamoto Ryōma|ja|坂本龍馬|12=draft}} was erected at Ushiyaguchi. Additionally, a short distance away lies Hirotani Cemetery, where the graves of successive abbots of Konpira Daigongen are located. The current {{ill|National Route 377|ja|国道377号|12=draft}} follows the course of this highway.

