Starting at an ichirizuka that once existed near the present {{ill|Okayama Omotecho Shopping Street|ja|岡山表町商店街|qq=draft}} (former Sen'ami Bridge), the route proceeds west through the Omotecho 3-chōme Shopping District, then heads south along {{ill|Yanagawa-suji|ja|柳川筋|qq=draft}}, turns west at the {{ill|Daionji Intersection|ja|大雲寺前停留場|lt=Daionji Intersection|lt_ja=大雲寺交差点|qq=draft}}, then continues southwest via the Daiku Intersection (in front of Okayama City Hall), passes to the west of the north side of {{ill|Ōmoto Station|ja|大元駅|qq=draft}}, and then heads south along the tracks of the {{ill|Uno Line|ja|宇野線|qq=draft}} toward Hayashima.<ref name="k-ourai" /> It is a direct route from the center of Okayama to Yukayama and Konpira. From Hayashima (at the Matsuosaka Intersection) onward, it coincides with the route described below for the Niwase Konpira Orai.

==== '''Niwase Konpira Orai''' ====
{| 
|-
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|2}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=173}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=21}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=162}}
|}
This is the route via the so-called {{ill|Kamogata Orai|ja|鴨方往来|qq=draft}}, which passes through many commercial districts opened in the city, and was used by those combining business with pilgrimage. In the former territory of {{ill|Kibi Province|ja|吉備国|qq=draft}} (Bizen, Bitchu, and Bingo), when referring to “Konpira Orai” the term in particular denotes this '''Niwase Konpira Orai'''. (However, it is not uncommon to refer to both this route and the later-described '''Kibitsu Konpira Orai''' collectively as “Niwase Konpira Orai”, since both routes use Niwase and Nudagawa in Kita Ward, Okayama City as their starting or relay points.) From the aforementioned ichirizuka at Omotecho (former Sen'ami Bridge) to the north side of Ōmoto Station, the path is identical to that of the Ōmoto–Meo Konpira Orai; however, on the latter route one proceeds straight west without turning at the intersection when heading south, passing through Kita-Nagase (near {{ill|Okayama Dome|ja|岡山ドーム|qq=draft}} and {{ill|Kita-Nagase Station|ja|北長瀬駅|qq=draft}}).

==== '''Kibitsu (Ichinomiya/Mashin) Konpira Orai''' ====
{| 
|-
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|53}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=27}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=42}}
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|180}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=389}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=245}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=151}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=162}}
|}
This route, intended purely for regional {{ill|Pilgrimage|ja|巡礼|qq=draft}} and for praying for a safe journey, not only covers Konpira and Yukayama but also incorporates the various {{ill|Ichinomiya|ja|一宮|1=Ichinomiya|qq=draft}} of Bizen and Bitchu—shrines that serve as the guardian deities of the journey. When viewed from the ichirizuka at former Sen'ami Bridge, the route ascends north in a direction diametrically opposed to the Ōmoto–Meo and Niwase Konpira Orais, following the former {{ill|Sanyōdō|ja|山陽道|qq=draft}} all the way to {{ill|Kibitsu|ja|吉備津|qq=draft}} in Okayama City.<ref name="k-ourai" /> On the Sanyōdō, after visiting {{ill|Kibitsu Hikon Shrine|ja|吉備津彦神社|qq=draft}} in the Ichinomiya area and then entering Kibitsu—after which one crosses the {{ill|Mashin Ichirizuka|ja|真金一里塚|qq=draft}} and turns south to reach {{ill|Kibitsu Shrine|ja|吉備津神社|qq=draft}}—the turning point is considered either the row of pine trees within the shrine grounds or, in some cases, Itakura-juku further to the west, where lanterns representing Konpira, Yukayama, and Kibitsu still remain. Just outside the torii on the southwest side of Kibitsu Shrine is a road marker directing pilgrims to Konpira, from which the true Kibitsu Konpira Orai begins. In this route the starting point of the Konpira Orai is considered to be the Sen'ami Bridge ichirizuka (the junction with the Okayama Domain Six Official Routes) when including a visit to Kibitsu Hikon Shrine, or the Mashin Ichirizuka (the junction with the former Sanyōdō) when including a visit to Kibitsu Shrine. Beyond Kibitsu Shrine, the route follows {{ill|Okayama Prefectural Route 389|ja|岡山県道389号|qq=draft}}, {{ill|Okayama Prefectural Route 245|ja|岡山県道245号|qq=draft}}, and then the municipal Kawairu Niwase Line of Okayama City southward to Niwase Orai (also known as Kamogata Orai), eventually reaching the Yanagawa marker at Nudagawa.<ref name="k-ourai" /> Many of the Konpira Orai markers in the Kibitsu area, including those near the starting point at Nudagawa, indicate both “Konpira-bound to the south/west toward Kurashiki/Tamashima” and “Kibitsu Shrine-bound to the north.” However, nowadays some consider the marker at {{ill|Ōtoku-ji Road Marker|ja|應徳寺の道標|qq=draft}} in Nudagawa, Kita Ward, to be the starting point—equating it with the Niwase Konpira Orai.

