== Overview ==
{{ill|Edo period|ja|江戸時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Edo period|qq=}}, as the faith in {{ill|Konpira Gongen|ja|金毘羅権現|lt=Konpira Daigongen|lt_ja=金毘羅大権現|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Konpira Gongen|qq=}}—the guardian deity of maritime traffic—spread among the common people, pilgrimages to Konpira became popular, and many devotees began visiting from all over the country. On the pilgrimage routes used by numerous travelers, lanterns and boundary stones were erected, and the roads were developed as highways.

From the mid‐Meiji period onward, as {{ill|Railway|ja|鉄道|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Railway|qq=}}s and {{ill|Automobile|ja|自動車|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Automobile|qq=}}s emerged, the Konpira Kaidō as a pedestrian route declined, and new roads following the course of the old highway were built. Today many of these roads have been designated as prefectural or national routes. Meanwhile, the roads formerly known as the “Konpira Kaidō” became classified as {{ill|Old Road|ja|旧道|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Old Road|qq=}}, and many lost their original appearance through regional road improvements, residential land development, and {{ill|Land Readjustment|ja|区画整理|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Land Readjustment|qq=}}.

In some areas once traversed by highways and traffic routes, remnants of the old road markers can still be seen. However, many of these markers were relocated during regional land development, and in such cases the actual orientation may not match that indicated by the marker—caution is advised. Moreover, many of these markers bear the inscription “sugu konpira,” which is to be interpreted as “directly to Konpira” (i.e. “proceed straight ahead toward Konpira”) rather than indicating physical proximity.

== '''Takamatsu Kaidō''' (高松街道) (also known as '''Kotohira Kaidō''' (琴平街道))
==
{| 
|-
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|32}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Kagawa|number=282}}
|}
It is also known as Kotohira Kaidō, Takamatsu Konpira Kaidō, among other names. Connecting Takamatsu Castle with various parts of Sanuki Province, the {{ill|Takamatsu Domain|ja|高松藩|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takamatsu Domain|qq=}}’s '''Sanuki Five Highways'''<ref>“Kotohira Kaidō” (prototype of {{ill|National Route 32|ja|国道32号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/National Route 32|qq=}}—the Takamatsu Mannō section, predecessor of {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 266 Chokushishitsu New Line|ja|香川県道266号勅使室新線|lt=Kagawa Prefectural Route 266|lt_ja=香川県道266号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 266 Chokushishitsu New Line|qq=}} and {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 282 Takamatsu-Kotohira Line|ja|香川県道282号高松琴平線|lt=Kagawa Prefectural Route 282|lt_ja=香川県道282号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 282 Takamatsu-Kotohira Line|qq=}} known as the '''main route'''); “Marugame Kaidō” (prototype of {{ill|National Route 11|ja|国道11号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/National Route 11|qq=}}—the Takamatsu–Marugame section, predecessor of {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 175 Kigake Gōhigashi Line|ja|香川県道175号衣掛郷東線|lt=Kagawa Prefectural Route 175|lt_ja=香川県道175号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 175 Kigake Gōhigashi Line|qq=}} and {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 33 Takamatsu-Zentōji Line|ja|香川県道33号高松善通寺線|lt=Kagawa Prefectural Route 33|lt_ja=香川県道33号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 33 Takamatsu-Zentōji Line|qq=}}); “Shido Kaidō” (prototype of the route linking the Takamatsu–Hakutori section of {{ill|National Route 11|ja|国道11号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/National Route 11|qq=}}—predecessor of {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 155 Mure New Line|ja|香川県道155号牟礼中新線|lt=Kagawa Prefectural Route 155|lt_ja=香川県道155号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 155 Mure New Line|qq=}}, and connecting from Hakutori via the predecessor of {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 1 Tokushima Hikida Line|ja|徳島県道・香川県道1号徳島引田線|lt=Kagawa Prefectural Route 1|lt_ja=香川県道1号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 1 Tokushima Hikida Line|qq=}} known as the “Awa Kaidō” linking Takamatsu and Tokushima domains); “{{ill|Nagao Kaidō|ja|長尾街道 (香川県)|lt=Nagao Kaidō|lt_ja=長尾街道|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nagao Kaidō|qq=}}” (predecessor of {{ill|Sanuki Higashi Kaidō|ja|さぬき東街道|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sanuki Higashi Kaidō|qq=}} and {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 10 Takamatsu-Nagao Ōuchi Line|ja|香川県道10号高松長尾大内線|lt=Kagawa Prefectural Route 10|lt_ja=香川県道10号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 10 Takamatsu-Nagao Ōuchi Line|qq=}}); and “Busshōzan Kaidō” (prototype of {{ill|National Route 193|ja|国道193号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/National Route 193|qq=}}—the Takamatsu–Shioe section, predecessor of {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 166 Iwasaki-Takamatsu Line|ja|香川県道166号岩崎高松線|lt=Kagawa Prefectural Route 166|lt_ja=香川県道166号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 166 Iwasaki-Takamatsu Line|qq=}} and {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 280 Takamatsu-Kagawa Line|ja|香川県道280号高松香川線|lt=Kagawa Prefectural Route 280|lt_ja=香川県道280号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 280 Takamatsu-Kagawa Line|qq=}}, connecting the “Shioe Kaidō” from Shioe Onsen) among the five highways.<ref>Refer to the brochure “Konpira Kaidō” (published by the Konpira Pilgrimage Guide Council).</ref> Its starting point is {{ill|Takamatsu Castle|ja|高松城 (讃岐国)|lt=Takamatsu Castle|lt_ja=高松城|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takamatsu Castle|qq=}} (Tamamo Castle); the outer moat was spanned by {{ill|Tokiwabashi (Takamatsu)|ja|常盤橋 (高松市)|lt=Tokiwabashi|lt_ja=常盤橋|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tokiwabashi (Takamatsu)|qq=}}, located near the present {{ill|Takamatsu Mitsukoshi|ja|高松三越|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takamatsu Mitsukoshi|qq=}}. From Kuririn, the route passes through such places as Enza, Hatada Jūsanzuka, Takimiya, Okada, and Enkei in the interior of Kagawa before reaching Kotohira. Because the {{ill|Matsudaira clan|ja|松平家|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Matsudaira clan|qq=}} of the Takamatsu Domain strongly protected Konpira Shrine, the highway was developed early on; the {{ill|Demba-sho|ja|伝馬|lt=Demba-sho|lt_ja=伝馬所|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Demba-sho|qq=}} was located at Kurikuma, while the starting-point {{ill|Ichirizuka|ja|一里塚|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ichirizuka|qq=}} on the Takamatsu side was placed at Kamimachi, Enza, Fukuiya, Tō, Ono, Okada, and Shijō—and the towns along the highway flourished as {{ill|Post town|ja|宿場|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Post town|qq=}}s.

The modern Takamatsu Kaidō comprises the current {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 266 Chokushishitsu New Line|ja|香川県道266号勅使室新線|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 266 Chokushishitsu New Line|qq=}} and {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 282 Takamatsu-Kotohira Line|ja|香川県道282号高松琴平線|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 282 Takamatsu-Kotohira Line|qq=}} (the former National Route 32) and its bypass ({{ill|National Route 32|ja|国道32号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/National Route 32|qq=}}), with portions overlapping the old highway. Between Enza and Kotohira, the {{ill|Takamatsu-Kotohira Electric Railway|ja|高松琴平電気鉄道|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takamatsu-Kotohira Electric Railway|qq=}} and its {{ill|Kotohira Line|ja|高松琴平電気鉄道琴平線|lt=Kotohira Line|lt_ja=琴平線|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kotohira Line|qq=}} run almost parallel.

In the Chūzan region, the primary river {{ill|Doki River|ja|土器川|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Doki River|qq=}} is known as Haraikawa in the midstream area where the Takamatsu Kaidō crosses it—said to be so because pilgrims on their way to Konpira Shrine purified themselves in this river.

