</gallery>

== '''Tadozu Kaidō''' ==
{| 
|-
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|319}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Kagawa|number=25}}
|}
A road from {{ill|Tadozu Port|ja|多度津港|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tadozu Port|qq=}} to [[:en:Zentsūji, Kagawa|Zentsūji]], Ikuno, Ōasa, and others leads to Kotohira. Like the Marugame Kaidō, it was frequently used by pilgrims arriving by Konpira ship; however, many of its users came from western Japan and Kyushu. It is said that during the [[:en:Tenpō|Tenpō era]], when the {{ill|Tadozu Domain|ja|多度津藩|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tadozu Domain|qq=}} undertook a major renovation of Tadozu Port, this route became even busier than the Marugame Kaidō.

The road between Tadozu and Kotohira was constructed as the Shikoku New Road by the politician {{ill|Ōkubo Kennojō|ja|大久保諶之丞|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ōkubo Kennojō|qq=}} during the Meiji period. The current {{ill|Kagawa Prefectural Route 25|ja|香川県道25号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kagawa Prefectural Route 25|qq=}} and [[:en:Japan National Route 319|National Route 319]] follow its course, running parallel to [[:en:Shikoku Railway Company|JR Shikoku]] and the [[:en:Dosan Line|Dosan Line]].

== '''Awa Kaidō''' ==
{| 
|-
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|32}}
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|438}}
|}
A road from [[:en:Tokushima Prefecture|Tokushima Prefecture]] crossing the [[:en:Sanuki Mountains|Sanuki Mountains]] to Kotohira. The name of Awa-chō within Kotohira Town derives from the large number of visitors from [[:en:Awa Province (Tokushima)|Awa Province]]. Although several highways exist connecting Awa and Sanuki Provinces, the route from [[:en:Miyoshi, Tokushima|Awa Ikeda]] (displayed as “Awa Ikeda”) via {{ill|Inonohana Pass|ja|猪ノ鼻峠|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Inonohana Pass|qq=}}, Zaitadogawa, and the Mominoki Pass to Kotohira is known as the Awa Betsu Kaidō. This highway was later developed as part of the Shikoku New Road by {{ill|Ōkubo Kennojō|ja|大久保諶之丞|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ōkubo Kennojō|qq=}} in the Meiji period and subsequently designated as [[:en:Japan National Route 32|National Route 32]]. Running parallel is the [[:en:Shikoku Railway Company|JR Shikoku]] Dosan Line.

Pilgrims from Awa, closely associated with Konpira Shrine, often combined their visit with that to {{ill|Hasakura-ji|ja|箸蔵寺|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hasakura-ji|qq=}} (known as Konpira Oku no-in). The highway connecting Konpira Shrine and Hasakura-ji—not via Inonohana from Zaitada but via Nikenchaya—is known as the Hasakura Kaidō. Although never developed as a vehicular road, the old route has been established as a hiking trail known as {{ill|Shikoku no Michi|ja|四国のみち|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikoku no Michi|qq=}}.

From [[:en:Tsurugi, Tokushima|Sadamitsu]] (displayed as “Sadamitsu”), across {{ill|Santō Pass|ja|三頭越|lt=Santō Pass|lt_ja=三頭峠|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Santō Pass|qq=}}, then through Kubo-ya, Zōda, Shijō, and on to Kotohira, most of the Awa Kaidō was later designated as [[:en:Japan National Route 438|National Route 438]]. Although the mountain pass section was slow to be modernized for automobiles, in 1997 the {{ill|Santō Tunnel|ja|三頭トンネル|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Santō Tunnel|qq=}} finally opened, easing vehicular traffic. In Mima Town, Mima City, a once-buried torii from the old highway has been revived through local residents’ efforts.<ref>[http://www.topics.or.jp/localNews/news/2011/05/2011_130628816198.html Revival of the Symbolic Torii in Mima Town] (Tokushima Shimbun, May 25, 2011)</ref>

== '''Iyo–Tosa Kaidō''' ==
{| 
|-
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|11}}
|{{Ja_Route_Sign|377}}
|}
A road from [[:en:Ehime Prefecture|Ehime Prefecture]] along the {{ill|Suihada Coast|ja|燧灘|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Suihada Coast|qq=}} to Toyohama, then inland via {{ill|Iyo Pass|ja|伊予見峠|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Iyo Pass|qq=}} to the Ushiyaguchi of Kotohira. In addition to pilgrims from [[:en:Iyo Province|Iyo]], many pilgrims from [[:en:Tosa Province|Tosa]]—arriving via the [[:en:Tosa Kaidō|Tosa (North) Kaidō]]—used this route. Many of the lanterns and offerings remaining along the highway were donated by the people of Iyo and Tosa. During the [[:en:Bakumatsu|Bakumatsu]] period, activists from Tosa (the [[:en:Shishi (Japan)|Samurai activists]]) also used this road, and a statue of [[:en:Sakamoto Ryōma|Sakamoto Ryōma]] was erected at Ushiyaguchi. Additionally, a short distance away lies Hirotani Cemetery, where the graves of successive abbots of Konpira Daigongen are located. The current [[:en:Japan National Route 377|National Route 377]] follows the course of this highway.

