|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=162}}
|}
This route, intended purely for regional [[:en:Pilgrimage|Pilgrimage]] and for praying for a safe journey, not only covers Konpira and Yukayama but also incorporates the various [[:en:Ichinomiya|Ichinomiya]] of Bizen and Bitchu—shrines that serve as the guardian deities of the journey. When viewed from the ichirizuka at former Sen'ami Bridge, the route ascends north in a direction diametrically opposed to the Ōmoto–Meo and Niwase Konpira Orais, following the former [[:en:San'yōdō|Sanyōdō]] all the way to {{ill|Kibitsu|ja|吉備津|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kibitsu|qq=}} in Okayama City.<ref name="k-ourai" /> On the Sanyōdō, after visiting [[:en:Kibitsuhiko Shrine|Kibitsu Hikon Shrine]] in the Ichinomiya area and then entering Kibitsu—after which one crosses the [[:en:Magane Ichirizuka|Mashin Ichirizuka]] and turns south to reach [[:en:Kibitsu Shrine (Bitchū)|Kibitsu Shrine]]—the turning point is considered either the row of pine trees within the shrine grounds or, in some cases, Itakura-juku further to the west, where lanterns representing Konpira, Yukayama, and Kibitsu still remain. Just outside the torii on the southwest side of Kibitsu Shrine is a road marker directing pilgrims to Konpira, from which the true Kibitsu Konpira Orai begins. In this route the starting point of the Konpira Orai is considered to be the Sen'ami Bridge ichirizuka (the junction with the Okayama Domain Six Official Routes) when including a visit to Kibitsu Hikon Shrine, or the Mashin Ichirizuka (the junction with the former Sanyōdō) when including a visit to Kibitsu Shrine. Beyond Kibitsu Shrine, the route follows {{ill|Okayama Prefectural Route 389|ja|岡山県道389号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Okayama Prefectural Route 389|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 岡山県道389号吉備津松島線}}, {{ill|Okayama Prefectural Route 245|ja|岡山県道245号|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Okayama Prefectural Route 245|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 岡山県道245号真金吉備線}}, and then the municipal Kawairu Niwase Line of Okayama City southward to Niwase Orai (also known as Kamogata Orai), eventually reaching the Yanagawa marker at Nudagawa.<ref name="k-ourai" /> Many of the Konpira Orai markers in the Kibitsu area, including those near the starting point at Nudagawa, indicate both “Konpira-bound to the south/west toward Kurashiki/Tamashima” and “Kibitsu Shrine-bound to the north.” However, nowadays some consider the marker at {{ill|Ōtoku-ji Road Marker|ja|應徳寺の道標|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ōtoku-ji Road Marker|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} in Nudagawa, Kita Ward, to be the starting point—equating it with the Niwase Konpira Orai.

==== Konpira Orai Beyond Nudge ====
{| 
|-
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=153}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=152}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=165}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=22}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=21}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=62}}
|{{Ja_Pref_Route_Sign|pref=Okayama|number=268}}
